// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Psychology is a science. Before we study the mind, let's learn how psychologists gather trustworthy evidence.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Experiments: manipulate an independent variable and measure a dependent variable, using random assignment to groups. Only experiments can show cause and effect.","Correlational studies: measure how two variables relate. A correlation coefficient (r) ranges from −1 to +1. Correlation does not prove causation.","Case studies, naturalistic observation, and surveys describe behavior."]},{"t":"key","text":"Random sampling helps results generalize to a population; random assignment controls confounding variables in experiments."},{"t":"p","text":"Bias can creep in through the placebo effect, experimenter bias (reduced by double-blind procedures), and social desirability bias in surveys."},{"t":"p","text":"Ethics: informed consent, protection from harm, confidentiality, and debriefing. Research is reviewed by an Institutional Review Board (IRB)."}]},{"say":"Everything you think and feel comes down to tiny cells sending signals. Let's see how neurons communicate.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The nervous system has the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system, which includes the somatic (voluntary movement) and autonomic systems. The autonomic system has the sympathetic division (fight-or-flight) and parasympathetic division (rest-and-digest)."},{"t":"key","text":"A neuron fires an action potential, an all-or-nothing electrical signal, down its axon. At the synapse, it releases neurotransmitters that bind to receptors on the next neuron."},{"t":"p","text":"Important neurotransmitters:"},{"t":"list","items":["Dopamine: reward and movement (linked to Parkinson's and schizophrenia).","Serotonin: mood, sleep and appetite (linked to depression).","Acetylcholine: muscle movement and memory (linked to Alzheimer's).","GABA: the main inhibitory neurotransmitter; glutamate: the main excitatory one.","Endorphins: pain relief."]},{"t":"p","text":"Drugs act as agonists (mimic or boost a neurotransmitter) or antagonists (block it). The endocrine system uses hormones like adrenaline and cortisol for slower, longer-lasting communication."}]},{"say":"Your brain has specialized regions that work together. Let's map the most important parts.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Brainstem: the medulla controls heartbeat and breathing; the reticular formation controls arousal.","Cerebellum: balance and coordination of movement.","Limbic system: the hippocampus forms new memories; the amygdala processes fear and emotion; the hypothalamus regulates hunger, thirst, temperature and the pituitary gland; the thalamus relays sensory information."]},{"t":"key","text":"The cerebral cortex has four lobes: frontal (planning, decisions, personality, motor cortex), parietal (touch, somatosensory cortex), occipital (vision) and temporal (hearing, language understanding)."},{"t":"p","text":"Language areas: Broca's area (frontal, speech production) and Wernicke's area (temporal, language comprehension). The left and right hemispheres specialize somewhat (lateralization), connected by the corpus callosum."},{"t":"p","text":"Neuroplasticity is the brain's ability to change and reorganize, especially in childhood. Tools like MRI, fMRI, EEG and PET scans let researchers study the brain."}]},{"say":"Biology also shapes how we sleep, sense the world, and inherit traits. Let's connect the pieces.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sleep cycles through stages about every 90 minutes. Non-REM stages include deep, slow-wave sleep (N3), important for growth and restoration. REM sleep has rapid eye movements, vivid dreams, and muscle paralysis; it's important for memory. Sleep disorders include insomnia, narcolepsy and sleep apnea. Circadian rhythms are the body's 24-hour cycles."},{"t":"p","text":"Sensation is detecting stimuli; perception is interpreting them. The absolute threshold is the minimum stimulus detected 50% of the time; the difference threshold (just-noticeable difference) follows Weber's law. Sensory adaptation reduces sensitivity to constant stimuli."},{"t":"key","text":"Behavior genetics uses twin and adoption studies to separate the effects of heredity (nature) and environment (nurture). Most traits result from both."}]}],[{"say":"Your brain doesn't just record the world; it actively builds your experience of it.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Bottom-up processing starts with sensory input; top-down processing uses expectations and experience to interpret it."},{"t":"p","text":"Perceptual set is a mental predisposition to perceive things a certain way. Context, culture and expectations shape perception."},{"t":"p","text":"Gestalt principles explain how we organize input into wholes: figure-ground, proximity, similarity, closure and continuity."},{"t":"p","text":"Depth perception uses binocular cues (retinal disparity, convergence) and monocular cues (relative size, interposition, linear perspective, texture gradient). Perceptual constancy lets us see objects as stable in size, shape and color despite changing input."},{"t":"p","text":"Attention is limited: selective attention focuses on some stimuli, and inattentional blindness and change blindness show how much we miss."}]},{"say":"How do we solve problems and make decisions? Often with shortcuts, and those shortcuts can lead us astray.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Concepts and prototypes help us organize information. Schemas are mental frameworks; we assimilate new information into them or accommodate them to fit new information."},{"t":"p","text":"Problem-solving strategies: algorithms (step-by-step procedures that guarantee a solution) and heuristics (mental shortcuts that are faster but error-prone)."},{"t":"key","text":"Cognitive biases distort thinking:"},{"t":"list","items":["Availability heuristic: judging likelihood by how easily examples come to mind.","Representativeness heuristic: judging by how well something matches a prototype.","Confirmation bias: seeking information that supports what we already believe.","Framing: the way an issue is presented affects decisions.","Functional fixedness and mental set: getting stuck in familiar ways of thinking."]},{"t":"p","text":"Creativity involves divergent thinking (generating many possible solutions), while convergent thinking narrows to one answer."}]},{"say":"Memory isn't a video recording. It's constructed, stored and retrieved, and it can change along the way.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The information-processing model: sensory memory → short-term (working) memory → long-term memory. Short-term memory holds about 7 ± 2 items for about 20 seconds; chunking increases capacity."},{"t":"key","text":"Encoding gets information in, storage keeps it, retrieval gets it out. Deep processing (thinking about meaning) creates stronger memories than shallow processing."},{"t":"list","items":["Explicit (declarative) memory: episodic (personal events) and semantic (facts).","Implicit (nondeclarative) memory: procedural skills and classical conditioning.","Retrieval is tested by recall, recognition and relearning."]},{"t":"p","text":"Why we forget: encoding failure, decay, and interference (proactive: old info disrupts new; retroactive: new info disrupts old). Memories can be distorted by the misinformation effect and source amnesia."},{"t":"p","text":"Study tips from research: spaced practice beats cramming, and testing yourself (retrieval practice) strengthens memory."}]},{"say":"What is intelligence, and can we measure it? Psychologists have debated this for over a century.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Theories of intelligence:"},{"t":"list","items":["Spearman's general intelligence (g): one underlying ability.","Gardner's multiple intelligences: separate types like musical, spatial and interpersonal.","Sternberg's triarchic theory: analytical, creative and practical intelligence.","Fluid intelligence (reasoning speed) tends to decline with age; crystallized intelligence (accumulated knowledge) tends to increase."]},{"t":"key","text":"A good test must be standardized (given the same way, compared to a norm group), reliable (consistent results) and valid (measures what it claims to)."},{"t":"p","text":"IQ scores form a normal distribution with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Stereotype threat can lower performance when people fear confirming a negative stereotype. Both genes and environment influence intelligence."}]}],[{"say":"Developmental psychology studies how we change from birth to old age, physically, mentally and socially.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Piaget's stages of cognitive development: sensorimotor (object permanence), preoperational (egocentrism, no conservation), concrete operational (conservation, logical thinking about concrete things) and formal operational (abstract reasoning)."},{"t":"p","text":"Vygotsky emphasized social learning: the zone of proximal development is what a child can do with help, and scaffolding is the support adults provide."},{"t":"p","text":"Attachment (Harlow's monkeys, Ainsworth's Strange Situation): secure attachment comes from responsive caregiving. Baumrind's parenting styles: authoritative (warm with clear rules, generally the best outcomes), authoritarian, permissive and neglectful."},{"t":"p","text":"Erikson described psychosocial stages, such as identity vs. role confusion in adolescence. In older adulthood, crystallized intelligence remains strong while processing speed declines."}]},{"say":"Much of what we learn comes from associations and consequences. Pavlov and Skinner discovered the rules.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Classical conditioning (Pavlov): a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned stimulus becomes a conditioned stimulus that triggers a conditioned response."},{"t":"p","text":"In Pavlov's experiment, food (US) caused salivation (UR). After pairing a bell (NS) with food, the bell alone (CS) caused salivation (CR). Extinction happens when the CS is repeatedly presented without the US; spontaneous recovery is the return of a weakened response after a pause."},{"t":"p","text":"Operant conditioning (Skinner): behavior is shaped by consequences."},{"t":"list","items":["Reinforcement increases behavior: positive (adding something pleasant) or negative (removing something unpleasant).","Punishment decreases behavior: positive (adding something unpleasant) or negative (removing something pleasant).","Schedules of reinforcement: variable-ratio schedules (like slot machines) produce high, steady responding that resists extinction."]}]},{"say":"We also learn by watching others and by thinking. You don't have to touch a hot stove to learn it burns!","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Observational learning (Bandura): we learn by watching models. In the Bobo doll experiment, children who watched an adult hit a doll were more likely to imitate the aggression."},{"t":"p","text":"Modeling works best when the model is similar, successful or admired. Vicarious reinforcement means we're more likely to imitate behavior we see rewarded."},{"t":"p","text":"Cognitive factors in learning:"},{"t":"list","items":["Latent learning (Tolman): learning that isn't shown until there's a reason to use it, like rats forming cognitive maps of a maze.","Insight learning (Köhler): sudden realization of a solution.","Learned helplessness (Seligman): after repeated uncontrollable bad events, an organism stops trying, even when escape is possible."]},{"t":"p","text":"Biological limits: taste aversions form after a single pairing because organisms are biologically prepared to learn some associations."}]}],[{"say":"Social psychology studies how other people affect what we think and do. The results are often surprising.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Fundamental attribution error: when explaining others' behavior, we overestimate personality and underestimate the situation."},{"t":"list","items":["Cognitive dissonance: discomfort from conflicting beliefs and actions, which we reduce by changing our attitudes.","Foot-in-the-door: agreeing to a small request makes people more likely to agree to a larger one.","Conformity (Asch): people often go along with a group's obviously wrong answer.","Obedience (Milgram): many participants obeyed an authority figure's orders to deliver what they believed were dangerous shocks.","Groupthink: the desire for harmony leads groups to make poor decisions.","Social loafing: people exert less effort in groups; social facilitation improves performance on easy tasks in front of others.","Bystander effect: people are less likely to help when others are present (diffusion of responsibility)."]}]},{"say":"What makes you, you? And what drives us to act? Personality and motivation theories offer different answers.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Personality perspectives:"},{"t":"list","items":["Psychodynamic (Freud): unconscious conflicts among the id, ego and superego; defense mechanisms like repression and projection.","Humanistic (Maslow, Rogers): growth toward self-actualization; unconditional positive regard.","Social-cognitive (Bandura): reciprocal determinism among behavior, thoughts and environment; self-efficacy."]},{"t":"key","text":"The trait approach's Big Five (OCEAN): Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism. These traits are fairly stable and measurable."},{"t":"p","text":"Motivation theories: drive-reduction (reduce needs like hunger), arousal theory (seek optimal arousal; Yerkes–Dodson law), and Maslow's hierarchy of needs. Intrinsic motivation comes from within; extrinsic from rewards."},{"t":"p","text":"Emotion theories: James–Lange (body reaction first, then emotion), Cannon–Bard (simultaneous), and Schachter–Singer two-factor (arousal plus a cognitive label)."}]}],[{"say":"Stress affects both the mind and the body. Psychology also studies what helps people thrive.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Stress is the process of responding to challenging events (stressors). Selye's general adaptation syndrome has three phases: alarm, resistance and exhaustion. Chronic stress can weaken the immune system."},{"t":"key","text":"Coping strategies: problem-focused coping (changing the stressor) and emotion-focused coping (managing your reaction). Social support, exercise, sleep and a sense of control all buffer stress."},{"t":"p","text":"Positive psychology studies well-being: gratitude, resilience, optimism and meaningful relationships. Post-traumatic growth describes positive changes after hardship."},{"t":"p","text":"The biopsychosocial model explains health and illness as the result of biological, psychological and social-cultural factors together."}]},{"say":"Psychological disorders are real, common and treatable. Understanding them reduces stigma.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Psychologists use the DSM-5-TR to diagnose disorders based on patterns of thoughts, feelings and behaviors that are distressing or interfere with daily life."},{"t":"p","text":"Major categories:"},{"t":"list","items":["Anxiety disorders: generalized anxiety, panic disorder, specific phobias, social anxiety.","Obsessive-compulsive disorder: unwanted obsessions and repetitive compulsions.","Trauma-related: PTSD, with flashbacks and hypervigilance.","Depressive disorders: major depressive disorder. Bipolar disorders: episodes of depression and mania.","Schizophrenia spectrum: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized thinking. Positive symptoms add experiences; negative symptoms remove normal functions.","Dissociative, personality, eating and neurodevelopmental disorders (like ADHD and autism spectrum disorder)."]},{"t":"key","text":"Most disorders arise from a mix of biological, psychological and social factors (the diathesis–stress model: a predisposition plus stressful experiences)."}]},{"say":"Many effective treatments exist, from talk therapy to medication. The best approach depends on the person and disorder.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Psychotherapies:"},{"t":"list","items":["Psychodynamic therapy: explores unconscious conflicts and past experiences.","Humanistic (client-centered) therapy: empathy, active listening and unconditional positive regard.","Behavioral therapies: exposure therapy and systematic desensitization treat phobias; token economies reward desired behaviors."]},{"t":"key","text":"Cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) helps people identify and change distorted thoughts and behaviors. It's one of the most widely supported treatments, especially for anxiety and depression."},{"t":"p","text":"Biomedical treatments:"},{"t":"list","items":["Antidepressants (like SSRIs, which increase serotonin), antianxiety drugs, mood stabilizers (like lithium for bipolar disorder), and antipsychotics.","Brain stimulation techniques like ECT for severe depression."]},{"t":"p","text":"Group and family therapy add social support. Treatment works best with a strong therapeutic alliance between client and therapist."}]}]];
export default text;
